> The vertices of a triangle are at points (0, 0), (-1, 1), and (3, 3). What is the area of the triangle?
[0mLet the vertices of the triangle be $A(0, 0)$, $B(-1, 1)$, and $C(3, 3)$.
We can use the determinant formula to find the area of the triangle.
The area of the triangle is given by
$$ \text{Area} = \frac{1}{2} |x_A(y_B - y_C) + x_B(y_C - y_A) + x_C(y_A - y_B)| $$
Substituting the coordinates of the vertices $A(0, 0)$, $B(-1, 1)$, and $C(3, 3)$, we have
$$ \text{Area} = \frac{1}{2} |0(1 - 3) + (-1)(3 - 0) + 3(0 - 1)| $$
$$ \text{Area} = \frac{1}{2} |0(-2) - 1(3) + 3(-1)| $$
$$ \text{Area} = \frac{1}{2} |0 - 3 - 3| $$
$$ \text{Area} = \frac{1}{2} |-6| $$
$$ \text{Area} = \frac{1}{2} (6) $$
$$ \text{Area} = 3 $$

Alternatively, we can use the determinant formula:
$$ \text{Area} = \frac{1}{2} \left| \begin{vmatrix} x_A & y_A & 1 \\ x_B & y_B & 1 \\ x_C & y_C & 1 \end{vmatrix} \right| = \frac{1}{2} \left| \begin{vmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\ -1 & 1 & 1 \\ 3 & 3 & 1 \end{vmatrix} \right| $$
Expanding the determinant along the first row, we get
$$ \text{Area} = \frac{1}{2} \left| 1 \cdot \begin{vmatrix} -1 & 1 \\ 3 & 3 \end{vmatrix} \right| = \frac{1}{2} |(-1)(3) - (1)(3)| = \frac{1}{2} |-3 - 3| = \frac{1}{2} |-6| = \frac{1}{2} (6) = 3 $$
Another method is to use the Shoelace Theorem.
The vertices are (0, 0), (-1, 1), and (3, 3).
$$ \text{Area} = \frac{1}{2} |(0 \cdot 1 + (-1) \cdot 3 + 3 \cdot 0) - (0 \cdot (-1) + 1 \cdot 3 + 3 \cdot 0)| $$
$$ \text{Area} = \frac{1}{2} |(0 - 3 + 0) - (0 + 3 + 0)| $$
$$ \text{Area} = \frac{1}{2} |(-3) - (3)| $$
$$ \text{Area} = \frac{1}{2} |-6| $$
$$ \text{Area} = \frac{1}{2} (6) = 3 $$

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{3}$
